THE CHANNEL THIS FAMILY MOST OBVIOUSLY WANTS, AND THE GATE IT LEAST OBVIOUSLY
NEEDS. Function summaries describe callees "whose source is not analyzed" --
dependencies, exactly where a user knows something hypergumbo cannot. But
taint.py already documents the asymmetry: a false "terminates" lets the walk
close a branch that is really open and DELETES A REAL SECURITY FINDING, while a
false "propagates" only leaves an unknown unknown.

So a user-supplied TERMINATING summary is a sanitizer declaration by another
name -- it removes a flow the tool would otherwise report -- and it now
qualifies a clean taint verdict with CAVEAT_USER_SUPPLIED_SANITIZER and its
exit-3 contract. A PROPAGATING one does not, because it cannot silence
anything. Granting this channel without the gate would have re-opened
INV-buzab's shape on a fresh surface: the tool answering "clean" on the strength
of the user's own word, with nothing saying so.

THE ATTRIBUTION HAD TO BE COLLECTED DURING THE WALK, and that is the part worth
reading. A SANITIZED flow still surfaces as a finding carrying
`sanitized_by_user_supplied`, which is how the existing sanitizer caveat knows
what to name. A TERMINATED branch produces NO FINDING AT ALL -- that is what
terminating means -- so if the walk does not record what it credited, nothing
downstream can. `_use_site_terminates` therefore gained a collector rather than
the verdict code gaining a query, following the `escape_sites` out-param already
in that same function.

PURELY ADDITIVE, which is what makes it safe to touch this path at all: the
bool return is untouched and the collector defaults to None, so an installation
where nobody wrote a summary produces byte-identical verdicts to before.
Recording happens only AFTER the line has actually terminated -- an entry
consulted on a line that then escapes was not credited with anything.

SCOPE, STATED IN THE CAVEAT'S OWN TEXT RATHER THAN ONLY IN A COMMIT MESSAGE:
the caveat is RUN-SCOPED, not flow-scoped. With no finding to attribute, it
rides every confirming TAINT verdict in a run where any user summary was
credited. That over-discloses, which is the safe direction -- the failure it
prevents is a clean verdict silently resting on a user's declaration -- but it
is coarser than the sanitizer caveat beside it and must not be read as per-flow
precision.

PROVENANCE IS STAMPED AT LOAD, NEVER READ FROM THE FILE, so a summary cannot
declare itself shipped. Otherwise a YAML key would be a way to silence a finding
AND suppress the disclosure of having silenced it. A test writes
`user_supplied: false` into a user file and asserts it still stamps True.

CONTROLS: shipped summaries are never marked user-supplied (all 113 of them, or
the caveat would fire every run and mean nothing); nothing is credited when the
line does not terminate; the collector is optional and changes no verdict when
absent; an explicit `search_dir` still means exactly that directory, so every
existing caller's fixtures are untouched; and a VIOLATED verdict never carries
the caveat -- the block sits under `if not violations:` and a test pins it
there, because finding evidence is trustworthy regardless of what went
unadjudicated.

TWO THINGS I GOT WRONG AND CORRECTED, recorded because both nearly changed the
outcome:
  * I reasoned that this needed a SCHEMA CHANGE to carry the attribution across
    an artifact boundary, and was close to deferring the whole arm on that. It
    is false: cmd_verify_claims runs the walk IN-PROCESS and hands
    taint_findings straight to the verdict logic, so there is no boundary.
  * My first test helper built a summary that does NOT terminate.
    `_summary_terminates` is a six-clause conjunction where any doubt reads as
    "no", so `param_to_return={}` alone is not enough -- it also needs
    `side_effect=True`. The predicate was being conservative exactly as
    designed; the helper's docstring now says so rather than the fix being
    silent.

